The fringe-width \(\beta\) in a medium with refractive index \(\mu\) is given by the formula:
\beta = \frac{\lambda_{\text{medium}} D}{d}
Where \(\lambda_{\text{medium}} = \frac{\lambda_{0}}{\mu}\) is the wavelength in the medium, \(\lambda_{0}\) is the wavelength in air, \(D\) is the distance to the screen, and \(d\) is the slit separation.
Given values:
• Wavelength in air, \(\lambda_{0} = 690 \text{ nm} = 690 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m}\)
• Refractive index of liquid, \(\mu = 1.44\)
• Slit separation, \(d = 1.5 \text{ mm} = 1.5 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}\)
• Distance to screen, \(D = 0.72 \text{ m}\)
Substitute these values into the formula:
\beta = \left(\frac{\lambda_{0}}{\mu}\right) \times \frac{D}{d} = \frac{690 \times 10^{-9} \times 0.72}{1.44 \times 1.5 \times 10^{-3}}
\beta = \frac{496.8 \times 10^{-9}}{2.16 \times 10^{-3}}
\beta = 230 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}
\beta = 0.23 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}
\beta = 0.23 \text{ mm}

The shift in the central fringe due to a transparent sheet is given by:
d\sin\theta = (\mu-1)t
For small angles, \(\sin\theta \approx \tan\theta = \frac{x}{D}\), where x is the fringe shift, D is the distance to the screen, and d is the slit separation.
d\left[\frac{x}{D}\right] = (\mu-1)t
Rearranging the formula to find the thickness t:
t = \frac{xd}{D(\mu-1)}
Given values:
• Slit separation \(d = 0.1\) cm
• Screen distance \(D = 50\) cm
• Refractive index \(\mu = 1.5\)
• Fringe shift \(x = 0.2\) cm
Substitute the values into the equation:
t = \frac{(0.2)(0.1)}{50(1.5-1)}
t = \frac{0.02}{50(0.5)}
t = \frac{0.02}{25}
t = 0.0008 \text{ cm}
Expressing in scientific notation:
t = 8 \times 10^{-4} \text{ cm}

\Delta x = \frac{7\lambda}{4}
\therefore \Delta \phi = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \Delta x = \frac{7\pi}{2}
Now intensity at given point is
I = 4I_{0} \cos^{2} \frac{\Delta \phi}{2} = 4I_{0} \cos^{2} \frac{7\pi}{4} = 2I_{0}
\therefore \frac{I}{4I_{0}} = \frac{1}{2}

Sol. 125
Given values:
• Wavelength \(\lambda = 5000 \mathring{A} = 5000 \times 10^{-10}\) m
• Slit separation \(d = 1.0\) mm \(= 1 \times 10^{-3}\) m
• Distance to screen \(D = 1.0\) m
The path difference \(\Delta x\) is given by:
\Delta x = d \sin \theta \quad \text{(i)}
For intensity, if \(I_0\) is the intensity due to a single slit, then the maximum intensity \(I_{max}\) in YDSE is:
I_{max} = (\sqrt{I_0} + \sqrt{I_0})^2 = (2\sqrt{I_0})^2 = 4I_0
The intensity \(I\) at any point is given by:
I = I_{max} \cos^2 \left(\frac{\Delta \phi}{2}\right)
We are looking for the distance from the center where intensity becomes half of the maximum intensity for the first time, i.e., \(I = \frac{I_{max}}{2}\).
\frac{I_{max}}{2} = I_{max} \cos^2 \left(\frac{\Delta \phi}{2}\right)
\cos^2 \left(\frac{\Delta \phi}{2}\right) = \frac{1}{2}
\frac{\Delta \phi}{2} = \frac{\pi}{4} \quad \text{or} \quad \frac{\Delta \phi}{2} = -\frac{\pi}{4}
For the first time the intensity becomes half, we consider the smallest positive phase difference:
\Delta \phi = \frac{\pi}{2}
The relation between path difference \(\Delta x\) and phase difference \(\Delta \phi\) is:
\frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \times \Delta x = \Delta \phi
Substituting \(\Delta \phi = \frac{\pi}{2}\):
\frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \times \Delta x = \frac{\pi}{2}
\Delta x = \frac{\lambda}{4}
From equation (i), for small angles (which is typical in YDSE), \(\sin \theta \approx \tan \theta = \frac{y}{D}\), where \(y\) is the distance from the center of the screen.
\Delta x \approx d \tan \theta \approx d \frac{y}{D}
Equating the two expressions for \(\Delta x\):
\frac{\lambda}{4} = d \frac{y}{D}
Solving for \(y\):
y = \frac{\lambda D}{4d}
Substitute the given values:
y = \frac{(5000 \times 10^{-10}) (1)}{4 \times (1 \times 10^{-3})}
y = \frac{5000 \times 10^{-10}}{4 \times 10^{-3}} = \frac{5000}{4} \times 10^{-10} \times 10^{3}
y = 1250 \times 10^{-7} = 125 \times 10^{-6}\text{ m}
The distance from the centre of the screen where intensity becomes half of the maximum intensity for the first time is \(125 \times 10^{-6}\) m.
The final answer is 125.

