Given data:
\lambda_1 = 800 \text{ nm} = 800 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m}
\lambda_2 = 600 \text{ nm} = 600 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m}
D = 7 \text{ m}
d = 0.35 \text{ mm} = 0.35 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}

### Step 1: Condition for coincidence of bright fringes

For the bright fringes of two wavelengths to coincide, their positions from the central maximum must be equal. The position of the \(n^{th}\) bright fringe is given by \(Y_n = \frac{n \lambda D}{d}\).
So, for coincidence:
n_1 \left(\frac{\lambda_1 D}{d}\right) = n_2 \left(\frac{\lambda_2 D}{d}\right)
Simplifying the equation:
\frac{n_1}{n_2} = \frac{\lambda_2}{\lambda_1}

### Step 2: Calculate the ratio of orders

Substitute the given wavelengths:
\frac{n_1}{n_2} = \frac{600 \times 10^{-9}}{800 \times 10^{-9}} = \frac{6}{8} = \frac{3}{4}
For the shortest distance from the central maximum, we take the smallest integer values for \(n_1\) and \(n_2\) that satisfy this ratio. Thus, \(n_1 = 3\) and \(n_2 = 4\).
This means the 3rd bright fringe of \(\lambda_1\) will coincide with the 4th bright fringe of \(\lambda_2\).

### Step 3: Calculate the shortest coincidence distance

Using the formula for the position of the bright fringe with \(n_1 = 3\) and \(\lambda_1 = 800 \text{ nm}\):
Y = \frac{n_1 \lambda_1 D}{d}
Y = \frac{3 \times (800 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m}) \times (7 \text{ m})}{0.35 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}}
Y = \frac{3 \times 800 \times 7 \times 10^{-9}}{0.35 \times 10^{-3}}
Y = \frac{16800 \times 10^{-9}}{0.35 \times 10^{-3}}
Y = 48000 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}
Y = 48 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}
Converting to millimeters:
Y = 48 \text{ mm}

y = n \frac{\lambda D}{d}
For the bright fringes due to both wavelengths to coincide, their positions from the central maximum must be equal:
y_1 = y_2
n_1 \lambda_1 \frac{D}{d} = n_2 \lambda_2 \frac{D}{d}
Since D and d are common for both wavelengths, we can simplify:
n_1 \lambda_1 = n_2 \lambda_2
Rearranging to find the ratio of the orders of the fringes:
\frac{n_1}{n_2} = \frac{\lambda_2}{\lambda_1} = \frac{550 \text{ nm}}{650 \text{ nm}} = \frac{11}{13}
For the least distance where the bright fringes coincide, we take the smallest integer values for \(n_1\) and \(n_2\) that satisfy this ratio. Thus, \(n_1 = 11\) and \(n_2 = 13\).
Now, we can calculate the position \(y\) using either wavelength. Let's use \(\lambda_1 = 650 \text{ nm}\) and \(n_1 = 11\).
Given values:
• \(\lambda_1 = 650 \text{ nm} = 650 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m}\)
• \(\lambda_2 = 550 \text{ nm} = 550 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m}\)
• \(d = 2 \text{ mm} = 2 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}\)
• \(D = 1.2 \text{ m}\)
y = n_1 \frac{\lambda_1 D}{d} = 11 \times \frac{(650 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m}) \times (1.2 \text{ m})}{2 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}}
y = 11 \times \frac{780 \times 10^{-9}}{2 \times 10^{-3}}
y = 11 \times 390 \times 10^{-6}
y = 4290 \times 10^{-6}
To express the answer in the required format \( \times 10^{-5} \) m:
y = 429 \times 10^{-5} \text{ m}

